Summary
The POST /api/v1/oauth/sociallogin endpoint authenticates users based entirely on client-supplied JSON and does not validate the identity token's signature, issuer, audience, or the binding between the supplied emailid / socialUId and a real Google/Firebase identity. Because the backend trusts the attacker-supplied identity, an unauthenticated remote attacker can:

    Fabricate a social-login request for any email address (verified against real, pre-existing accounts such as admin@chobani.com) and receive a valid authorizationCode.
    Exchange that code for a fully signed server-issued session (bmt JWT cookie + bvt + CSRF + device tokens) - no credentials, no valid Google token, no MFA.
    Read the victim's complete profile (PII: name, DOB, gender, mobile, postal code, state, marketing prefs).
    Write / modify the victim's profile via POST /api/v1/user/update - demonstrated end-to-end with persistence.

This is a critical broken-authentication flaw leading to full account takeover with read AND write access to any Chobani user account.
Affected Endpoints
Endpoint	Method	Purpose
/api/v1/oauth/sociallogin	POST	Issue authorization code for attacker-chosen identity
/api/v1/oauth/token	POST	Exchange code → session (JWT + cookies + CSRF/device tokens)
/api/v1/user/details	GET	Read victim profile (authenticated)
/api/v1/user/update	POST	Modify victim profile (authenticated)
Root Cause

    The social-login request body carries identity fields - firstName, lastName, userName, emailid, socialUId, issuer, audience, active, isadmin - that are trusted verbatim by the server.
    There is no server-side token validation (no Google/Firebase signature, issuer, or audience verification) and no proof-of-ownership for the emailid.
    The token-exchange endpoint then mints a server-signed RS256 JWT (bmt) whose user.eml claim equals the attacker-chosen email, producing a fully legitimate session.

Steps to Reproduce

    Compute the PKCE challenge for verifier TESTVERIFIER123456789012345678901234567890: challenge = base64url( SHA-256( verifier ) ) → xgOSPH29r2e6mYbHGnOsDas3tQa1uJLF-f1QySSFp2E.
    POST /api/v1/oauth/sociallogin with the attacker-chosen victim emailid and an arbitrary socialUId (no real Google token sent) → returns authorizationCode.
    POST /api/v1/oauth/token (same cookie jar) with that code + the verifier → returns bmt/bvt/rft cookies plus csrftoken and devicetoken.
    Call GET /api/v1/user/details (read) and POST /api/v1/user/update (write) as the victim.

